<Base title="Pulse" description="From raw light to heartbeat: an explorable built on a night of my own wearable data.">
  <h1>Pulse</h1>
  <p><em>[Interactive, Phase 4]</em></p>
  <h2>1 · A night in my signals</h2>
  <p>My ring records heart rate and heart-rate variability every five minutes, movement every thirty seconds, and estimated sleep stages. Here is one night of it. <span class="tk">[chart: HR / HRV / movement / sleep stages, scrubbable; night picker]</span></p>
  <h2>2 · Where a heart rate comes from</h2>
  <p>A ring or watch doesn't hear your heart. It shines light into your skin and measures how much comes back. Each heartbeat pushes a little more blood through the vessels, so the reflected light dips and rises. The ring stores summaries rather than this raw waveform, so the trace below is <strong>reconstructed from my own heart-rate and HRV values</strong>.</p>
  <h2>3 · Now move</h2>
  <p>Add footsteps and arm swing and the pulse drowns. <span class="tk">[controls: add motion · band-pass cutoff slider · artifact removal on/off · detected beats → BPM]</span> This is the problem I worked on at Valencell.</p>
  <h2>4 · Optional: try it with your own pulse</h2>
  <p>If you'd like, rest a fingertip over your phone's camera, or sit still in front of your webcam. The camera turns on only when you press the button. Processing happens entirely in your browser, and no video is recorded or sent anywhere. <span class="tk">[button: Start camera]</span></p>
  <p><small>This is a demonstration, not a medical device. Camera-based pulse readings are noisy and are known to be less accurate on darker skin tones.</small></p>
</Base>
